/* The morning brief: the shortlist, and now shaped like one. It was a single serif
   paragraph at 1.46rem and it was not being read -- five sentences each carrying a claim, a
   number, a superlative and a subordinate clause, run together with nothing to tell a
   skimming reader which one was about what. Prose was the wrong form for facts that share
   no argument, so each fact takes a row and says in the margin what kind of fact it is.
   Down a step in size from the paragraph it replaces: at this width a line is now read
   whole rather than swum through, and the block's job is to be glanced at.
   Its links are underlines rather than coloured text: five accent-blue phrases in five
   lines reads as a menu, which is the shape this block exists to stop being. The rule
   thickens on hover, so the page still says plainly that every subject here is one click
   from what it is a claim about. */
.brief{list-style:none;margin:.2rem 0 2.5rem;padding:0;max-width:64em;
       display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.7rem}
/* The label is a fixed rail, so five kickers form a column a reader can run down rather
   than five differently-indented starts. It collapses below the text on a narrow viewport,
   where a 9rem rail would leave the sentence six words wide. */
.brief li{display:grid;grid-template-columns:8.5rem 1fr;gap:.1rem .9rem;
          align-items:baseline}
.brief .bk{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.66rem;text-transform:uppercase;
